karr #37 made _normalize_bool die on a hashref/arrayref where a boolean
was expected, deliberately: that is the same class of bad data #37 was
about, and failing beats the old wrong-but-quiet "every reference is true".
The Changes entry states it.

What it does not do is say what went wrong:

  $k8s->struct_to_object("Pod", { spec => { hostNetwork => {} } });
  # Not a SCALAR reference at lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm line 132.

Neither the field nor the class appears. The consumer gets a line number
inside IO::K8s and has to bisect their own manifest to find which boolean
carried the bad value -- and on an inflated manifest that value came from
JSON they may not have written.

"Fail loud" is the house rule, and this is loud. It is not diagnostic,
which is the other half of the same rule. The deref on
lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm:132 is the only place that can die here, so:

  die "Bool value must be a scalar or scalar ref, got " . ref($value)
      if ref($value) && ref($value) !~ /\A(SCALAR|REF)\z/ && !blessed(...)

or the equivalent -- the exact shape is open. Blessed scalar refs
(JSON::PP::Boolean, boolean.pm, Types::Serialiser) must keep working.

Naming the attribute needs the caller to pass it: the Bool coercer knows
the attribute name at _k8s() time, and IO::K8s::_inflate_struct knows
$class and $attr. Worth deciding whether the message carries just the type
or the field too -- the field is what actually helps, and _inflate_struct
is where most bad data arrives.

Wants a test asserting the message content, not just that it dies.

Found reviewing 7309437e (karr #37).

--- VERIFIED 2026-08-18 against 4887f658 (full suite green: 56 files, 3979 tests) ---

Reproduced exactly as reported:

  $k8s->struct_to_object('Pod', { spec => { hostNetwork => {} } });
  # Not a SCALAR reference at lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm line 132.

Same message for an arrayref and for a coderef. Line 132 is still the only
place that can die: _normalize_bool's `$value = $$value if ref $value`.

CORRECTION to the ticket -- only the INFLATION path is anonymous. The
constructor path already names the attribute, because Moo wraps a failing
coercion:

  IO::K8s::Api::Core::V1::SecurityContext->new(privileged => {});
  # coercion for "privileged" failed: Not a SCALAR reference at
  #   lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm line 132.

So the work is not "make _normalize_bool talk more" everywhere. It is:
  (a) make the message itself say what kind of value was rejected, so both
      paths read alike, and
  (b) give the _inflate_struct caller (lib/IO/K8s.pm:931, the is_bool
      branch) the class + JSON field name that Moo supplies for free on the
      constructor path. That branch is where manifest data arrives, and it
      is the one that currently says nothing.

SECOND DEFECT, found while verifying, same function, same shape as karr #37
-- a ref-to-ref is SILENTLY TRUE, it does not die:

  IO::K8s::Resource::_normalize_bool(\0)     # 0   correct
  IO::K8s::Resource::_normalize_bool(\\0)    # 1   WRONG, and quiet

`$value = $$value if ref $value` dereferences exactly once; \\0 yields \0,
which is still a reference and therefore truthy. This is karr #37's "every
reference is true in Perl" one level deeper, and it is the silent half of
this ticket rather than the loud half. Any check added here must classify
the POST-deref value, not only the pre-deref one -- a fix that only inspects
ref($value) before the deref leaves \\0 returning true.

Must keep working (measured, all return 0/1 correctly today):
  JSON::PP::Boolean / JSON::MaybeXS booleans, \0 and \1,
  any blessed scalar ref -- bless \(my $x = 0), 'Whatever'  => 0

WHAT TO DO
1. lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm:130-136 -- after the deref, reject a value that is
   still a reference, and name the type in the message. Exact wording open;
   it has to distinguish "not derefable at all" (HASH/ARRAY/CODE) from
   "derefed to another reference" (\\0), because the second one is currently
   wrong rather than merely undiagnostic. Blessed SCALAR/REF must pass.
2. lib/IO/K8s.pm:931 -- the is_bool branch of _inflate_struct. It holds
   $class and $attr; the message must carry them. Nothing else in
   _inflate_struct dies with context today, so decide whether this is a
   local eval/rethrow or a $context argument on _normalize_bool -- prefer
   whichever leaves the coercer's own message unchanged for the constructor
   path, since that one is already good.
3. is_array_of_bool shares the function via the array coercer
   (lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm:264). A bad element dies there with the same
   anonymous message. Decide whether the element index appears; at minimum
   it must not regress.
4. Tests into t/53_bool_normalization.t, which already owns the boolean
   claims from karr #37. Assert the MESSAGE TEXT, not just that it dies, and
   add \\0 as its own case in both directions (struct_to_object and the
   constructor) -- it currently passes silently and would otherwise stay
   uncovered.

